SEG = 'ELG'
CAT = 'cbcsEligibility'
Q = [
("At registration, which copy of the patient's insurance card should be kept?",
 'Front and back', ['Front only', 'Back only', 'None if photo ID is shown'],
 'The back of the card usually carries the claims address, payer phone numbers and other details the biller needs, so both sides are scanned or copied.'),
("Why is a returning patient's coverage checked before every visit?",
 'Coverage can change at any time', ['Payers reset deductibles monthly', 'It renews any referral on file', 'Payers require it for each claim'],
 'Jobs, plans and eligibility change between visits. Checking coverage each time catches terminated or changed plans before the claim is sent.'),
('Deductible $1,000 with $400 met, then 20% coinsurance. Allowed amount is $900. What does the patient owe?',
 '$660', ['$180', '$600', '$720'],
 'She pays the $600 left on her deductible, then 20% of the remaining $300 ($60), for $660. $180 is 20% of the whole $900.'),
('Deductible met. The plan pays 80% of the allowed amount, which is $450. How much does the plan pay?',
 '$360', ['$90', '$400', '$450'],
 "With the deductible met, the plan pays 80% of $450, which is $360. The patient's 20% coinsurance is the other $90."),
("Which payment does not count toward a plan's out-of-pocket maximum?",
 'Monthly premiums', ['Deductible payments', 'Coinsurance payments', 'Copays for covered care'],
 'Per HealthCare.gov, deductibles, copays and coinsurance for covered in-network care count toward the out-of-pocket maximum. Premiums, non-covered services and out-of-network care do not.'),
('Approval a payer gives before a planned service is performed is a:',
 'Preauthorization', ['Referral', 'Predetermination', 'Coordination of benefits'],
 "Preauthorization (prior authorization) is the payer's approval before the service. A referral comes from the primary care provider; a predetermination only estimates benefits."),
('A service was preauthorized. What does that approval usually not guarantee?',
 'That the claim will be paid', ['That the request was reviewed', 'That an auth number was issued', 'That the payer was notified'],
 'Payers state that an authorization is not a guarantee of payment. The claim can still deny for eligibility, benefit limits, coding or filing errors.'),
('Which plan type pays a share of charges for any provider the member chooses, with no network?',
 'Indemnity plan', ['HMO', 'EPO', 'Point-of-service plan'],
 'Traditional indemnity (fee-for-service) insurance reimburses a share of covered charges from any provider. HMOs and EPOs limit members to a network.'),
('Which plan uses a primary care gatekeeper but still pays something for out-of-network care?',
 'Point-of-service plan', ['Exclusive provider plan', 'Indemnity plan', 'Staff model HMO'],
 'A point-of-service plan blends HMO and PPO features: members pick a primary care provider for referrals and can go out of network at a higher cost share.'),
('A health savings account must be paired with which kind of plan?',
 'High-deductible health plan', ['Medigap plan', 'Copay-based HMO', 'Indemnity plan with low deductible'],
 'An HSA is available only to people covered by a qualifying high-deductible health plan, so the plan type matters when estimating what the patient pays at the visit.'),
('A new patient says he has no insurance. What should registration offer first?',
 'Medicaid screening', ['Full payment before the visit', 'Higher self-pay rates', 'Referral to a collector'],
 'Uninsured patients may qualify for Medicaid or other programs, sometimes retroactively. Screening first can turn a self-pay balance into a covered claim.'),
('When should a fixed office visit copay be collected?',
 'At the time of service', ['After the claim is paid', 'On the first statement', 'When the remittance posts'],
 'A copay is a set amount the plan says the patient pays per visit, so it is known up front and collected at check-in or checkout.'),
("Why compare the patient's photo ID with the insurance card at check-in?",
 'To catch identity theft', ['To find the payer ID number', 'To confirm her copay amount', 'To learn her plan type'],
 "Matching a photo ID to the card helps stop someone from using another person's coverage. A photo that does not match the person is a red flag under the FTC's Red Flags Rule."),
("Before billing workers' compensation for a work injury, which detail does the office need?",
 "Carrier's claim number", ["Patient's group number", 'A referral from her PCP', 'A signed ABN'],
 "Workers' compensation claims are tied to the carrier's claim number for that injury. The group number belongs to the patient's health plan, which is not billed for the injury."),
('A fixed dollar amount the patient pays for each office visit is the:',
 'Copayment', ['Coinsurance', 'Deductible', 'Allowed amount'],
 'A copayment is a flat amount per service. Coinsurance is a percentage of the allowed amount, and the deductible is paid before the plan starts paying.'),
('Billed $400, allowed $300, deductible met, 20% coinsurance. What does the patient owe?',
 '$60', ['$80', '$100', '$240'],
 'Coinsurance applies to the allowed amount, not the charge: 20% of $300 is $60. The $100 above the allowed amount is a contractual adjustment for an in-network provider.'),
('A practice offers a prompt-pay discount to self-pay patients. What keeps it consistent and defensible?',
 'A written policy applied to all', ['Offering it case by case', 'Approving it by the front desk', 'Limiting it to regular patients'],
 'A written financial policy applied the same way to every eligible patient avoids arbitrary pricing and the appearance of inducements.'),
('Which detail on an insurance card tells the clearinghouse where to route the electronic claim?',
 'Payer ID', ['Group number', 'Member ID', 'Rx BIN'],
 'The payer ID identifies the receiving payer for electronic claims. The group and member IDs identify the plan and person, and the Rx BIN routes pharmacy claims.'),
('Under the ACA, plans that cover dependent children must offer that coverage until the child turns:',
 '26', ['19', '21', '23'],
 'Under 45 CFR 147.120, a group health plan or issuer that offers dependent coverage of children must make it available until the child reaches age 26.'),
("Which detail must the office confirm before billing a dependent's visit to a parent's plan?",
 'That the child is covered', ['That the child lives at home', "The child's school enrollment", "The parent's copay history"],
 "Eligibility verification confirms the dependent is actually covered on the subscriber's plan on the date of service. A dependent not on the plan will deny however clean the claim is."),
]
